#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106667} :activity/id #uuid "9012f3b0-be7a-525d-829c-8f6b0ce85627" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106667 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107088 :tutorial/content "Consider the standard basis $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\big\\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors.\n\nLet $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ be a matrix of a linear transformation $\\mathbf T.$ We can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=2i \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this transformation, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/61/61fc1f3f1e76ff49b9967399d1b3a3a63bbe9845a59f2b2841af6e14510b2a7a.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation represented by the matrix $T$ multiplies the vector $\\mathbf{i}$ by a factor of $2$ and leaves the vector $\\mathbf j$ unchanged. This type of transformation is called a **stretch in the $\\mathbf x$-direction.** We can also call it a **horizontal stretch.** In this case, we have a **horizontal stretch of scale factor** $\\mathbf 2.$\n\nIn general, a stretch in the $x$-direction of scale factor $k$ with its center at the origin is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2b251d5e-1184-5f44-b4ff-ba8c7a4fa35d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3538 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "24a98178-6804-578c-bcca-7be62c1fba55" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106757}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106668 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105764 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3105e8eb-010f-542b-9e40-12fda6ea00e7"} :step/id #uuid "c1790453-592c-524b-85e4-b8f01da73075" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106670}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106669 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be42e4b3-4b15-589c-a7e1-23cc07083aff"} :step/id #uuid "07af67d3-64da-5097-8283-afbf2a41a98e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106671}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107089 :tutorial/content "Consider the standard basis $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\big\\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors.\n\nLet $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0.5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ be a matrix of a linear transformation $\\mathbf T.$ We can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0.5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=i \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0.5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=0.5i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this transformation, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/aa/aa646019f6af73e795661060a1a678b5b3be7961d7e60e3ae601d911033382d2.png)\n\nThe transformation represented by the matrix $T$ adds $0.5\\mathbf{i}$ to $\\mathbf j,$ while the vector $\\mathbf i$ remains unchanged. This type of transformation is called a **horizontal shear.** In this case, we have a **horizontal shear with a shear factor of** $\\mathbf{0.5}.$\n\nIn general, a horizontal shear of scale factor $k$ with its center at the origin is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4c09cece-483e-5612-b070-e22441b1fce3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3541 :tutorial/title "Horizontal Shear"} :step/id #uuid "951b2248-fe6b-5eff-813b-d09a530ec2c3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106758}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106671 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c36ce626-1562-51f8-b3fd-d7dc7ce8659c"} :step/id #uuid "d1d718ce-70f6-584e-8355-6694712167b3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106672}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106672 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69bac3fa-d409-5c16-87b2-383423e8d754"} :step/id #uuid "d6fd3589-d3b4-5da7-894d-cd35f040c1ef"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106757 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107101 :tutorial/content "Now, let $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ be a matrix of a linear transformation $\\mathbf T.$ We can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=i \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this transformation, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/19/19b1f75508ae7fc0255edcf2821bb26df352f46544add7740957291d9cb8e117.png)\n\nThe transformation represented by the matrix $T$ multiplies the vector $\\mathbf{j}$ by $2$ and leaves the vector $\\mathbf i$ unchanged. This type of transformation is called a **stretch in the $\\mathbf y$-direction.** We can also call this a **vertical stretch**. In this case, we have a **vertical stretch of scale factor** $\\mathbf 2$. \n\nIn general, a stretch in the $y$-direction of scale factor $k$ with its center at the origin is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1e893b6a-17a2-5f2b-8a94-e7237e811fe5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4414 :tutorial/title "Vertical Stretch"} :step/id #uuid "22da4aa0-ee23-5b88-a006-73210316cdce" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106668}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106758 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107102 :tutorial/content "Now, let $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ be a matrix of a linear transformation $\\mathbf T.$ We can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0.5\\end{bmatrix}=i+0.5j \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following image:\n\n![](images/66/6626afefeea861f7a4d20e251021ed8db264f365051b85b619770293ac638b37.png)\n\nThe transformation represented by the matrix $T$ adds $0.5\\mathbf{j}$ to $\\mathbf i,$ while the vector $\\mathbf j$ remains unchanged. This type of transformation is called a **vertical shear.** In this case, we have a **vertical shear with a shear factor of** $\\mathbf{0.5}.$\n\nIn general, a vertical shear of scale factor $k$ with its center at the origin is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ k & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f7ec9a33-b7de-5d47-a628-944e05516d52" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4415 :tutorial/title "Vertical Shear"} :step/id #uuid "403a0824-e79f-5e4b-8f2d-3d571d56e116" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106669}}] :activity/title "Shear and Stretch as Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105991}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186050231 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105764 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105616 :question/id #uuid "ffbe74ea-33e6-5998-8692-ed5d7bd0c30d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5450" :question/problem "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that ![](images/0a/0adb71786ea5de6203b155882b20000488d5467cf6dfcd34e6fa4e06ec091e46.png) and ![](images/94/94fb0fd9708c343c89fd98ff5b87c901c9e795026cc1dbd35d29cff92d6fe59e.png) what is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we are told that $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $x$-component of $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ is $4$ times the $x$-component of $\\mathbf{v},$ while\n\n- the $y$-components of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ are the same.\n\nSo, we conclude that $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a stretch of scale factor $4$ in the $x$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n![](images/77/779a10caf7c07cbe4b5891240892db5433adc0f9f8895192a3a8bed5f9301126.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3105e8eb-010f-542b-9e40-12fda6ea00e7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106489 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0436fb77-220b-55d9-94ff-28050a7ea87b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345386} {:answer/id #uuid "61e3050c-9571-53ce-97a2-bfa1efb0fcf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346203} {:answer/id #uuid "a309bde0-2c99-5310-98cf-9ccb1a1c4411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346764} {:answer/id #uuid "a3a56973-ac38-5743-84a0-d1ef38040b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346769} {:answer/id #uuid "c62bfcb2-2e69-58a1-9204-8e5b2385569a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347074} :answer-field/id #uuid "18fa7b6b-56a1-5e0c-89c4-207b3876954d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347650}] :question/id #uuid "b9beb837-2a5f-5628-9a45-88a8085c9d8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-80725" :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(v)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we are told that $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $v$ and $T(v),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $x$-components of $v$ and $T(v)$ are the same, while\n\n- the $y$-component of $T(v)$ is $\\frac{1}{3}$ times the $y$-component of $v.$\n\nSo, we conclude that $T$ represents a stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{3}$ in the $y$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89f93f56-ac75-57b5-95c1-f90f361f5930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105089} {:answer/id #uuid "b33af94e-420f-5cfd-b0af-f118b8697055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105090} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6bae65-78c2-5856-9f0b-163e1c048ff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105091} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0d689b-1c5e-51c2-88cc-ac6b5d900a1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105092} {:answer/id #uuid "2aff7b31-5277-574c-afb7-f649464edb91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105093}] :answer-field/id #uuid "637d172d-46f4-5b21-8f12-e315dda47530" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105088} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c397e37-1d42-5efe-81d5-6ff323b2d323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394052} {:answer/id #uuid "6f6ed60d-2840-53c0-a19c-73a1c38e3ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394053} {:answer/id #uuid "38883822-19a8-50c5-ac70-21dd818b00e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394054} {:answer/id #uuid "75955132-8432-5b46-8ff2-1f131bf31dd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394055} {:answer/id #uuid "48d10010-6d30-5ed7-a764-671a0140e320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394053} :answer-field/id #uuid "78374675-6ad5-5e77-a243-9ee8ff3a2a6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394051}] :question/id #uuid "85f5eecb-79b7-5107-8347-e7d15fcb457b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80726" :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(v)=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we are told that $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $v$ and $T(v),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $x$-component of $T(v)$ is $2$ times the $x$-component of $v,$ while\n\n- the $y$-components of $v$ and $T(v)$ are the same.\n\nSo, we conclude that $T$ represents a stretch of scale factor $2$ in the $x$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299626 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0af2365a-62fe-5480-b5c5-f59d7ec237a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297609} :answer-field/id #uuid "81bb8a6d-03ac-5652-9d14-ab2897453ae6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299075} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6090f03a-9580-5ed1-bf89-5192ae656219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297961} :answer-field/id #uuid "deeca449-31b9-5807-b94e-ae403782e58c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299392} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f5f9717-60b9-509d-8f02-5a722352ed7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297801} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8cc1bf7-0f3a-589e-b2eb-b952e2a45879" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299479}] :question/id #uuid "8da8bcfe-6c86-5e96-b6b0-a1747142aaaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-317606" :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $u=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we are told that $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $u$ and $T(u),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $y$-components of $u$ and $T(u)$ are the same, while\n\n- the $x$-component of $T(u)$ is $3$ times the $x$-component of $u.$\n\nSo, we conclude that $T$ represents a stretch of scale factor $3$ in the $x$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299627 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc0d324b-2a31-526e-8a3a-3c4e4b38e8c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298632} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f072330-ba6e-5938-952f-5081cc759f4c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299066} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "290a2819-162a-5f03-b3cd-fb2209164d3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297725} :answer-field/id #uuid "939e7506-cc69-5ea4-8cbf-ba29431d0b5d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299125} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "214588c1-072d-5bcf-99eb-221f8bd8de6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297693} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3527308-48a5-5983-a9e7-8ec0fe38a538" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299243}] :question/id #uuid "9ae414af-8983-5296-9cdd-42629db05cdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-317609" :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $u=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we are told that $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $u$ and $T(u),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $y$-components of $u$ and $T(u)$ are the same, while\n\n- the $x$-component of $T(u)$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$ times the $x$-component of $u.$\n\nSo, we conclude that $T$ represents a stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $x$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c88e1bb-0f7d-5446-ab7e-094f413dc36d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297739} :answer-field/id #uuid "782f96a1-f160-5dc8-8433-85339b6ff9ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299044} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b2d8acc-3202-599e-9fff-0c2a8b24bd8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297935} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae76415e-9d77-5a96-9667-9fb18df63307" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299226} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "430ea936-fda5-545d-97bf-f45fa237d597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297822} :answer-field/id #uuid "c076d607-2027-5b9e-9631-b7691bc50fa1" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299283} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddbcf6d5-b530-5103-87a8-441bb13f846e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298502} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc09f504-e547-5ef4-8baf-7ad481ddb326" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299382}] :question/id #uuid "0f51d731-7249-537a-abb9-1fe5a0fa8533" :question/math-academy-id "q-317612" :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $u=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix},$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we are told that $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $u$ and $T(u),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $x$-components of $u$ and $T(u)$ are the same, while\n\n- the $y$-component of $T(u)$ is $\\sqrt{3}$ times the $y$-component of $u.$\n\nSo, we conclude that $T$ represents a stretch of scale factor $\\sqrt{3}$ in the $y$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Matrix of a Stretch Transformation Given a Vector and its Image"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105765 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105617 :question/id #uuid "83ef303a-c25c-5cd5-8ab7-2c14cb05d433" :question/math-academy-id "e-5454" :question/problem "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ of $\\,\\mathbb{R}^2.$ If $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation such that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{i})=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{j})=\\mathbf{j}$ then, what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}.$\n\n![](images/db/db15a65fd944c24ea2545baea08e6a0789b749295f623d8d4fdc2f07a37d4b73.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{i})=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{j})=\\mathbf{j},$ we can draw the image of the square under the transformation $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/1e/1e5310f50dab35bc25447af9b37d972e11eda0afee98ac99b87a819ba62f56a1.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a vertical shear with a shear factor of $2.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be42e4b3-4b15-589c-a7e1-23cc07083aff" :knowledge-point/questions [{: